<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1878 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hatikvah</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">הַתִּקְוָה</bdi> / <bdi lang="he-Latn">Hatikvah</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hebrew poem from 1878 about hope, set to an old European tune, that became the national anthem of Israel.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Naftali Herz Imber</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Samuel (Shmuel) Cohen, who set the poem in 1887 or 1888 to a melody he knew from Romania; the tune family goes back to &quot;La Mantovana&quot;, attributed to Giuseppe Cenci (Giuseppino del Biado), c. 1600</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Zionist Jewish communities; the State of Israel</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Iași, Romania (first version); Złoczów (Zolochiv), Austrian Galicia, present-day Ukraine; set to music in Rishon LeZion, Ottoman Palestine</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem first written 1877, text dated 1878, published 1886 in Barkai; set to music 1887/1888</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Naftali Herz Imber (written 1877–1878, published 1886; he died in 1909))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Naftali Herz Imber, a Jewish poet from Złoczów (Zolochiv) in Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, wrote the first version of his poem in 1877 while staying with a Jewish scholar in Iași, and its text is dated 1878. The nine-stanza poem, &quot;Tikvatenu&quot; (&quot;Our Hope&quot;), set out his thoughts after the founding of the settlement of Petah Tikva and expressed the wish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the diaspora to return to the Land of Israel. Imber emigrated to Ottoman-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> in 1882 and read the poem to the pioneers of the early Jewish villages, among them Rishon LeZion, Rehovot, Gedera and Yesud <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>'ala. It was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1886 in his first book, Barkai.</p><p>In 1887 Shmuel Cohen, a resident of Rishon LeZion aged about 17 or 18, saw how the farmers responded to the poem and sang it to a melody he knew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>; he later recalled it as &quot;Carul cu boi&quot; (&quot;The Ox Cart&quot;). The tune belongs to a family that goes back to &quot;La Mantovana&quot;, an Italian song of about 1600, which circulated across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under many names and which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedrich-smetana">Bedřich Smetana</a> used in &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vltava">Vltava</a>&quot; from Má vlast. Cohen's setting, in a minor key unusual for an anthem, carried the poem quickly through the Zionist communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. It was taken up as an anthem by Hovevei Zion and then by the Zionist movement.</p><p>The Zionist Organization held anthem competitions in 1898 and 1900 and chose none of the entries, but Imber's poem was already popular: a session of the Fifth Zionist Congress in Basel in 1901 closed with it, and at the Sixth Congress in 1903 it was sung by delegates opposed to the proposal of a Jewish state in Uganda, who wanted a homeland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. The Eighteenth Zionist Congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> formally adopted &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a>&quot; as the movement's anthem in 1933. The British Mandate government briefly banned its public performance and broadcast from 1919, in response to a rise in Arab anti-Zionist political activity. A former member of the Sonderkommando reported that Czech <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> sang it at the entrance to the gas chamber of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auschwitz-concentration-camp">Auschwitz-Birkenau</a> in 1944, and were beaten by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waffen-ss">Waffen-SS</a> guards as they sang.</p><p>When the State of Israel was established in 1948, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a>&quot; was proclaimed its national anthem unofficially. It became the official anthem in November 2004, when the Knesset sanctioned an abbreviated and edited version in an amendment to the Flag and Coat-of-Arms Law, since renamed the Flag, Coat-of-Arms, and National Anthem Law. The official text keeps only the first stanza and an amended refrain of Imber's poem.</p><p>The anthem has also met objections. Some religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> criticised it for naming neither God nor the Torah; Rabbi Abraham Isaac Kook wrote an alternative, &quot;HaEmunah&quot; (&quot;The Faith&quot;), while still endorsing the original. Some non-Jewish citizens of Israel, particularly Arab Israelis, object that its words speak only of Jewish aspirations, and several public figures, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> minister Salah Tarif, the Muslim minister Raleb Majadale and Supreme Court justice Salim Joubran, have declined to sing it; proposals to change the text have been raised from time to time. Later performances and arrangements include Barbra Streisand's in 1978 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-williams-missionary">John Williams</a>'s adaptation for the film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 2005.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A poem of longing written for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> living in exile: as long as the hope of return survives in Jewish hearts, the hope of becoming a free people in their own land is not lost. Its mood is hopeful and determined, though the tune it is sung to is in a minor key. The official anthem text is a single long sentence that sets out a condition and then its outcome.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כָּל עוֹד בַּלֵּבָב פְּנִימָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">kol 'od balevav penimah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As long as within the heart, inside,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">נֶפֶשׁ יְהוּדִי הוֹמִיָּה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">nefesh yehudi homiyyah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a Jewish soul yearns,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וּלְפַאֲתֵי מִזְרָח קָדִימָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">ulefa'atei mizrakh kadimah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and toward the edges of the east, forward,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">עַיִן לְצִיּוֹן צוֹפִיָּה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">'ayin letsiyyon tsofiyyah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>an eye looks out to Zion,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">עוֹד לֹא אָבְדָה תִּקְוָתֵנוּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">'od lo avedah tikvatenu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our hope is not yet lost,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הַתִּקְוָה בַּת שְׁנוֹת אַלְפַּיִם</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">hattikvah bat shenot alpayim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the hope two thousand years old,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לִהְיוֹת עַם חָפְשִׁי בְּאַרְצֵנוּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">lihyot 'am khofshi be'artsenu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to be a free people in our land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">אֶרֶץ צִיּוֹן וִירוּשָׁלַיִם</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">erets tsiyyon virushalayim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the land of Zion and Jerusalem.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: The anthem text, after the poem by Naftali Herz Imber (1878). Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%94%D7%AA%D7%A7%D7%95%D7%95%D7%94" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>This is the anthem's text (two stanzas), not Imber's 1878 poem 'Tikvatenu', whose first stanza and refrain differ (from memory, to be checked against the 1886 printing: the refrain spoke of returning to the land of our fathers, the city where David encamped). The credit line should say so.</li><li>line 1.2: 'homiyyah' is literally 'murmurs, stirs, moans'; rendered 'yearns'.</li><li>line 1.3: 'pa'atei mizrach' is literally 'the corners/edges of the east'; 'kadimah' is both 'eastward' and 'forward'.</li><li>Zion and Jerusalem stay as names in en; romanized as tsiyyon, yerushalayim (here with the prefix: virushalayim).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="QM6D2D0lnt0" data-title="Hatikvah: Israel Philharmonic Orchestra and the Tel-Aviv Philharmonic Choir, conducted by Leonard Bernstein"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Israel Philharmonic Orchestra and the Tel-Aviv Philharmonic Choir, conducted by Leonard Bernstein</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Leonard Bernstein - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QM6D2D0lnt0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Stars Salute Israel at 30 (Barbra Streisand)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1978</span><span class="sg-chain-h">performance</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Munich (film, score by John Williams)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2005</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Come Down (Anderson .Paak)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sampled in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hatikvah (Daniel Sa'adon, with a dabke rhythm)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2018</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hope (Omer Adam and Noa Kirel, produced by Scott Storch)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2021</span><span class="sg-chain-h">pop remix with added English words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">HaEmunah (Abraham Isaac Kook)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">proposed alternative anthem</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La Mantovana (Giuseppe Cenci, &quot;Fuggi, fuggi, fuggi da questo cielo&quot;)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1600</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the melody family</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Carul cu boi (Romanian song)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the melody Cohen said he knew</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ezekiel 37 (Vision of the Dry Bones)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible biblical allusion in the refrain</span></li><li><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1797</span><span class="sg-chain-h">named as inspired by it</span></li></ul></section>
